HTML pages currently become entries from root runtime work or fragments. Direct const root inserts supply builder metadata. A general `$entry` purpose and capability-checked `io.set_title` remain queued. See @../progress (Progress) and @../progress/packages-and-builders (Packages and builders).

This page describes entry metadata and its relationship to project configuration.

### The `@project` namespace

`@project` is an explicitly bound immutable namespace. The accepted `$project`
directive publishes its predefined fields only. Today's grouped record also
publishes supported extra metadata until that configuration cutover.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @project version

    footer #= [: v[version]]
]

Normal project modules and eligible project-owned support packages may bind it.
It is never implicitly injected or directly re-exported. Project-package
exposure restrictions and field provenance still apply. Config helper and input
bindings do not become project metadata merely because they exist.

See @#project-config (Project configuration) for the field contract and
@#build-inputs (Build configuration values) for independent input resolution.

### HTML builder slots

Use direct top-level const inserts on an active root to supply document metadata:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Moth]
    #[$in description:A language for creating reliable software]
    #[$in lang:en]
    #[$in favicon:[@assets/favicon.svg]]
    #[$in stylesheets:[@styles/site.css]]
    #[$in head:<meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff">]
    #[: <main>Hello Moth</main>]
]

The HTML builder registers `title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon`,
`body_style`, `stylesheets` and `head`. Ordinary top-level const and runtime
fragments render into the body. A nested or aliased `$in` remains a template
composition helper, not a builder message. Helper files and imported roots do
not contribute to the selected entry.

Repeated `title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon` and `body_style`
contributions concatenate in source order. Template body whitespace remains
part of each value, including a space immediately after `:`. A supplied empty
value counts as present. An absent title uses the route, then project name, with the configured
title prefix and postfix. An absent description emits no description element.
Absent language, favicon and body style use the HTML builder defaults.

The builder escapes title text and attribute values. It inserts `head` as raw
HTML after its dedicated head content. It removes only repeated complete head
contributions that render identically in the page's URL context. It does not
rewrite duplicates inside one contribution.

Each `stylesheets` contribution must contain exactly one tracked CSS resource
and optional formatting whitespace. Plain paths, external URLs, site-root
values, non-CSS resources and combined resources are invalid. The builder
deduplicates by stable resource origin, keeps the first occurrence and renders
the URL relative to each output page. Use `head` for an external stylesheet.

Resource and site-root pieces retain their identity through folding and output
planning. Named contributions keep their tracked resources live, including on
metadata-only pages. The builder does not scan rendered HTML for paths. The
former `page_title`, `page_description`, `page_lang`, `page_favicon`,
`page_body_style` and `page_head` names are ordinary constants with no implicit
document effect.

### Future entry purpose and runtime title

General `$entry` purpose validation and `io.set_title` remain queued. They do
not replace builder slots or change the current active-root policy.

